WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.460
Good to have you on fascinating frontiers, episode 61,

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.080
coming to you on May 2, 2026.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.280
Here's your space and science briefing.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.860
NASA's lithium-fed nuclear thruster

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.460
has been tested for the first time,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.560
advancing options for Mars missions.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.300
A laser communications terminal provided high-definition views

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.340
during the Artemis 2 mission around the moon.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.980
This technology allowed millions to follow

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.420
the crew's 10-day journey in greater detail

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.920
than previous radio systems.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.040
The system enabled the transmission of much clearer footage

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.140
than what we saw in earlier missions.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.820
Data travels via a narrow beam of light

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.260
at the speed of light requiring extreme alignment precision.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.280
This approach represents a significant step forward

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.080
in how we communicate with spacecraft

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.420
on deep space missions.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.820
The success during Artemis 2 highlights the potential

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.160
for future lunar and planetary exploration.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.940
To understand the remarkable engineering

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:56.100
behind this capability, we have a special deep dive

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.260
into how laser communications actually work from space.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.140
If we scale the distance from Earth to the moon

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:05.580
down to just one kilometer, the challenge becomes clear.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.460
A laser communication beam would still need to hit a receiver

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.320
the size of a small coin while both the spacecraft

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.520
and the ground station move at orbital speeds.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.000
The process starts with the spacecraft

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.100
modulating data onto a narrow beam of light.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.280
That beam travels through space

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.040
at 300,000 kilometers per second

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.840
to reach a ground-based telescope.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.560
This method supports data rates high enough

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.020
for live, high-definition video streams,

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.320
which is a big improvement over radio frequency systems.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.380
During the Artemis 2 mission,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.380
this made it possible for millions

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.920
to watch detailed footage of the crew's journey.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.420
Alignment requires extreme accuracy using advanced tracking

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.640
systems to handle the motion and atmospheric effects.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.160
One remaining challenge is maintaining reliable performance

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.920
when the line of sight passes close to the sun

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.400
where interference might disrupt the signal.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.940
NASA has conducted the first test

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:03.840
of a lithium-fed nuclear thruster.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.660
This next-generation system could enable more efficient

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.740
propulsion for human journeys to Mars.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.560
The thruster uses nuclear power to heat

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.580
and expel lithium propellant for greater efficiency

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:16.880
on long-duration flights.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.500
The successful test validates the basic concept

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.600
and opens the door to future spacecraft integration.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.900
This development brings a novel propulsion concept

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.540
to life in the lab environment.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.360
It promises greater efficiency for long-duration space travel.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.380
This could significantly shorten the time required

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:36.500
for missions to Mars.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:40.220
Engineers are now looking toward integrating this technology

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.420
into actual spacecraft designs.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.120
The first-ever test of a lithium-fed nuclear thruster

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.580
has brought this concept to life in a controlled setting.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.280
Nuclear power heats the lithium propellant,

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.480
which is then expelled to generate thrust.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.440
This approach offers much greater efficiency

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.400
compared to traditional chemical rockets for long trips.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.100
The test confirmed that the basic idea

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.680
works as expected under lab conditions.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.380
With this validation, the focus shifts to making it ready

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:10.660
for real space missions.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:13.340
It could make journeys to Mars much quicker

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:14.620
than current methods allow.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:18.320
The question now is how soon we might see this thruster

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.580
powering an actual spacecraft beyond Earth orbit.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.940
The Federal Aviation Administration has indicated

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:28.480
a net date of May 12th for the next starship test flight.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.240
This approval clears the regulatory path

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.740
for SpaceX's ongoing vehicle development.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.400
The test flight represents the next phase

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.640
in the vehicle's development.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.520
Regulatory clearance like this is essential

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.340
for continuing the test program.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.340
It shows that the project is moving ahead as planned.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.700
The focus now shifts to the actual launch preparations.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:52.080
We can expect more updates as the data approaches.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.500
Following the successful Artemis II lunar flyby,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.580
experts are outlining the next steps in the program.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:00.920
The focus is on building infrastructure

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.000
for a sustained human presence on the moon and beyond.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.840
These plans focus on creating the conditions

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.480
for longer-term operations on the lunar surface.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.080
The goal is to move beyond short visits

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.600
to something more permanent.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.240
This phase will involve more robotic

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.760
and eventually crewed missions to the moon.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:20.980
It builds directly on the achievements

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.560
of the Artemis II flight.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.120
The strategy emphasizes collaboration

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:27.820
and sustainable practices for the future.

